er/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-15-(-20)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/example-5466.json" "file_sha256" "eb2b20cec64e2a67dcce4a9d01d69e7560df65c1b696ab6df4e7ab879a48459d" "basis" 1362 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10),$ and $(-4,-3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n\n- $(4,-3)$\n\n- $(-1,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/example-5467.json" "file_sha256" "69ad7d91e5bf570d1077130951795da1ef390aa58d1c389993305fd25bc5035c" "basis" 1362 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/example-5467.json" "file_sha256" "69ad7d91e5bf570d1077130951795da1ef390aa58d1c389993305fd25bc5035c" "basis" 1362 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-159975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-159975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(3)-(5)(1)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-317509" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317509" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "45"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317509" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317509" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-18"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6-(-2)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-12271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(-1)-(1)(1)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-317507" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317507" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317507" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317507" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\nv={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the inverse of $T$:\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(5)(1)-(6)(2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo the original vector is\n\n$$\nv=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-317443" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317443" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{12}{7}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317443" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-317443" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{7}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(7,5),$ and $(2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(0,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{9-8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(1,1),(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-159978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(0,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(-1,0),(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(1,3)$\n- $(3,3)$\n- $(2,3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,2),(3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-9262" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9262" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-9262" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $Q$ is mapped to the quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,4),(-4,4),(12,4),$ and $(7,3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $Q?$\n\n- $(-2,4)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(4,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${Q}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $Q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(-2,4),(-4,4),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-12275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-12275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,4),(0,2),$ and $(7,10).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(2,3)$\n- $(1,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,1),(-1,2),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "297617636209854754aff53cdf74b5802853bf90b163cb191c7d1806d947cbb9"} {"question" "q-159977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "q-159977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/state.json" "file_sha256" "63da8bfda6af9c63542c2c8b7502482cfb6cfa4ee88dfd0217f98a2afd066c87"} {"question" "e-5466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{al